[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Cloud Databases: Google Cloud SQL and Spanner"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial explores Google Cloud SQL and Spanner, two powerful cloud-based database services offered by Google Cloud Platform (GCP). We'll delve into their features, use cases, and how to get started with each."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Google Cloud SQL"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Google Cloud SQL is a fully managed relational database service for MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server. It simplifies database setup, maintenance, and administration, allowing you to focus on application development. It's a great choice for web applications, mobile backends, and other applications that require a relational database."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Features of Cloud SQL"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fully managed: Google handles backups, replication, patching, and maintenance."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scalable: Easily scale your database instance up or down to meet your application's needs."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Highly available: Cloud SQL offers high availability configurations to minimize downtime."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Secure: Data is encrypted at rest and in transit, and Cloud SQL integrates with Google Cloud's security features."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Easy to use: Simple web interface and command-line tools for managing your databases."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Getting Started with Cloud SQL"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"1. **Create a Google Cloud Project:** If you don't already have one, create a new project in the Google Cloud Console."},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"2. **Enable the Cloud SQL API:** In the Google Cloud Console, navigate to the API Library and enable the Cloud SQL Admin API."},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"3. **Create a Cloud SQL Instance:** Go to the Cloud SQL page in the Google Cloud Console and click 'Create Instance'. Choose your database engine (MySQL, PostgreSQL, or SQL Server), instance ID, region, and other settings. For this example, we'll use MySQL."},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"4. **Configure the Instance:** Set the root password, machine type, and storage capacity. You can also configure networking settings to allow access from your application."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"5. **Connect to the Instance:** Once the instance is created, you can connect to it using a MySQL client or other database tool. You'll need the instance's public IP address and the root password."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"mysql -h <INSTANCE_IP> -u root -p"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Replace `<INSTANCE_IP>` with the public IP address of your Cloud SQL instance."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Example SQL Operations (MySQL)"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"CREATE DATABASE mydatabase;
USE mydatabase;

CREATE TABLE users (
  id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  name VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
  email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE
);

INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('John Doe', 'john.doe@example.com');
INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('Jane Smith', 'jane.smith@example.com');

SELECT * FROM users;"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Backups and Recovery"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cloud SQL automatically performs backups. You can also create on-demand backups. To restore from a backup, simply select the backup from the Google Cloud Console."},{"order":23,"type":"heading","text":"Google Cloud Spanner"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Google Cloud Spanner is a globally distributed, scalable, strongly consistent database service. It combines the benefits of relational databases (SQL, ACID transactions) with the scalability and availability of NoSQL databases. Spanner is ideal for applications that require high availability, strong consistency, and global scale, such as financial services, supply chain management, and gaming."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Features of Spanner"},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Globally distributed: Data is replicated across multiple regions for high availability and low latency."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Strongly consistent: Spanner provides ACID transactions and strong consistency, ensuring data integrity."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scalable: Spanner can scale to handle massive amounts of data and traffic."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SQL support: Spanner supports standard SQL queries, making it easy to migrate existing applications."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Automatic sharding: Spanner automatically shards data across multiple servers, simplifying database management."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Getting Started with Spanner"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"1. **Create a Google Cloud Project:** If you don't already have one, create a new project in the Google Cloud Console."},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"2. **Enable the Spanner API:** In the Google Cloud Console, navigate to the API Library and enable the Cloud Spanner API."},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"3. **Create a Spanner Instance:** Go to the Spanner page in the Google Cloud Console and click 'Create Instance'. Choose your instance ID, region, and processing capacity."},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"4. **Create a Spanner Database:** Once the instance is created, you can create a database. Specify the database name and schema."},{"order":36,"type":"subheading","text":"Example SQL Operations (Spanner)"},{"order":37,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE Singers (
  SingerId INT64 NOT NULL,
  FirstName STRING(1024),
  LastName STRING(1024),
  SingerInfo BYTES(MAX),
) PRIMARY KEY (SingerId);

CREATE TABLE Albums (
  SingerId INT64 NOT NULL,
  AlbumId INT64 NOT NULL,
  AlbumTitle STRING(1024),
) PRIMARY KEY (SingerId, AlbumId),
  INTERLEAVE IN PARENT Singers ON DELETE CASCADE;

INSERT INTO Singers (SingerId, FirstName, LastName) VALUES (1, 'Marc', 'Richards');
INSERT INTO Singers (SingerId, FirstName, LastName) VALUES (2, 'Catalina', 'Smith');

INSERT INTO Albums (SingerId, AlbumId, AlbumTitle) VALUES (1, 1, 'Total Junk');
INSERT INTO Albums (SingerId, AlbumId, AlbumTitle) VALUES (1, 2, 'Go, Go, Go');
INSERT INTO Albums (SingerId, AlbumId, AlbumTitle) VALUES (2, 1, 'Green');

SELECT s.FirstName, s.LastName, a.AlbumTitle
FROM Singers AS s
JOIN Albums AS a ON s.SingerId = a.SingerId
WHERE s.SingerId = 1;"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"Note the use of `INT64` instead of `INT` and `STRING(1024)` instead of `VARCHAR`. Spanner has its own data types."},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"Data Modeling in Spanner"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spanner's data modeling is crucial for performance.  Interleaving tables (using `INTERLEAVE IN PARENT`) is a key technique for optimizing queries that frequently join related data.  Careful consideration of primary keys is also important for efficient data access."},{"order":41,"type":"heading","text":"Cloud SQL vs. Spanner: When to Use Which?"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cloud SQL is a good choice for applications that require a relational database and don't need global scale or strong consistency across multiple regions. It's also more cost-effective for smaller workloads."},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spanner is the right choice for applications that require global scale, strong consistency, and high availability. It's more expensive than Cloud SQL, but it offers unparalleled performance and reliability for demanding workloads."},{"order":44,"type":"paragraph","text":"In summary, choose Cloud SQL for most standard relational database needs. Choose Spanner when you absolutely require global scale and strong consistency, and are willing to pay the higher price."}]
